Figure 3: Refiner performance across corruption severities. Bars show mean DSC (a) and mean HD95
(b) across seven anatomy–dataset pairs (Liver, Pancreas, Spleen from MSD; Liver, Pancreas, Spleen from
AbdomenCT1K; Spleen from AMOS22); error bars show the sample standard deviation across those seven
pair-level values. Seven refiners are compared per severity group: raw upstream (grey), morphological (dark
grey), PostDAE (red), RAM (purple), U-Net w/o RAG (amber), GRACE-U (blue-green), and GRACE-M
(yellow-green). TTAug requires repeated upstream inference and is therefore not defined for the precomputed
synthetic-corruption inputs in this figure.
PostDAE.
PostDAE decreases the DSC on every tier. The largest loss is on TotalSegmentator (−0.487
DSC, +10.2 mm HD95). PostDAE replaces the input with its reconstruction through a fixed latent prior.
Thus, it projects each mask onto its learned manifold. On an almost correct input, this projection removes
correct detail. This explains the largest loss on the strongest upstream. PostDAE was also designed for
2D chest X-ray masks. We apply it slice by slice to 3D abdominal organs, and nothing keeps the slices
consistent. This effect is largest on HD95. The row thus shows the effect of a single-anatomy 2D shape prior
on volumetric multi-organ data. This is the gap that our work addresses.
Comparison of the variants.
The three variants achieve comparable aggregate performance but differ in
their behaviour on severely degraded inputs. Consistent with Table 1, GRACE-M produces the larger mean
lift on the mid and most of strong tiers, while all three variants have small non-negative mean changes on
the strongest segmentor, TotalSegmentator. Mechanistically, GRACE-U is a single-pass feedforward network
and produces more conservative corrections when little reliable signal is available; GRACE-M refines in a
frozen latent space and can synthesise anatomically plausible completions guided by the retrieved exemplar.
GRACE-M-D stays within 0.01 DSC of GRACE-M and needs about seven times more compute (Table 6).
Its value is the uncertainty estimate for each sample, and it does not increase the accuracy.
