Held-out modality.
For each modality in {US, MRI, CT}, we remove all its datasets. No case of that
modality enters the index at test time. Ultrasound is the clearest case. CAMUS has the same three cardiac
structures as ACDC, so the anatomy stays almost constant.
The imaging physics, the speckle and the
sector geometry change. The MRI holdout is more difficult. Some MRI anatomies, such as the hippocampal
subregions, do not occur in the CT and ultrasound data. Thus, the modality and part of the anatomy are
both unseen.
Refinement transfers from CT to MRI, but it loses most of its in-distribution gain (Table 3, Block B). An
MRI-free GRACE-U refiner with an index of 713 CT livers increases the DSC of CHAOS MRI liver from
0.515 to 0.599 (+0.084). On the same CHAOS MRI queries, the refiner trained with MRI gains +0.349.
Refinement does not transfer to ultrasound. At an input DSC of 0.438, the US-free refiner decreases the
DSC to 0.236. The refiner trained with ultrasound reaches 0.772. Retrieval still selects the correct structure
for all 101 CAMUS queries. However, the shape agreement between a query and its retrieved pool drops to
29% of the in-modality value for ultrasound. It stays at 83% for CT and 73% for MRI. We did not evaluate
refinement for the CT holdout, so Table 3 reports only the MRI and US holdouts. Appendix C explains the
low ultrasound value.
Neither experiment shows zero-shot generalisation. We present transfer to unseen anatomies and modalities
as an open research direction. An important question is the minimum number of support cases.
4.5
Retrieval analysis
We first quantify the contribution of the retrieval strategy to refinement performance and then evaluate the
retrieved neighbours independently of the refiner.
Retrieval strategy.
Table 4 evaluates the retrieval strategy with the trained GRACE-M refiner held
fixed. The corrupted input without refinement is the baseline; a random same-class peer already recovers
roughly 40% of the oracle gap, indicating that the anatomically conditioned prior is useful even before any
similarity ranking. Selecting the shape-aware K-NN adds a further lift, and the oracle row upper-bounds
the retrieval contribution. The gap between the random and K-NN rows measures the combined benefit
of similarity-based ranking and the projected retrieval space over random same-class selection; it does not
isolate the projector. The projector-versus-raw comparison in Table 5 isolates that contribution by holding
the candidate set and ranking protocol fixed.
