As shown in Table 3, our LeViT-UNet++ achieves a competitive overall DSC of 76.57% 
and HD95 of 18.29, with only 8.03M parameters and an inference FPS of 242. Compared 
with its predecessor LeViT-UNet, our LeViT-UNet++ obtains a clear performance gain 
(+2.88% overall DSC) while reducing the parameter count from 15.90M to 8.03M and 
maintaining comparable inference speed. Among all models with less than 10M 
parameters, LeViT-UNet++ achieves the best HD95 metric and competitive overall DSC. 
Specifically, our method yields strong results on the Aorta, Liver and Spleen. Although 
heavy-weight hybrid models such as H2Former and UCTransNet deliver higher overall 
DSC, they suffer from substantially larger parameter overhead. Meanwhile, ultra-light 
CNN baselines like CMUNeXt run much faster but exhibit obvious degradation on HD95, 
showing inferior boundary segmentation capability. 
