(2) Results on MSD-Heart dataset 
We evaluate LeViT-UNet++ on the MSD-Heart dataset for heart segmentation. As 
shown in Table 6, our method obtains a DSC of 88.76, HD95 of 5.20, NSD of 27.67, and 
ASD of 1.29. Even though nnUNet, UNet++, UXNet, MedNeXt-v1 and Swin UNETR attain 
higher DSC values, many of them suffer from degraded HD95 and NSD boundary metrics. 
nnFormer reaches only 4.91 in NSD with a drastically reduced DSC of 52.85, which is not 
applicable for cardiac segmentation. Relative to the LeViT-UNet baseline, LeViT-UNet++ 
improves the DSC from 84.26 to 88.76 alongside consistent gains on boundary metrics. 
Mobile U-ViT performs poorly on both DSC and boundary measurements. This evidence 
confirms that our LeViT-UNet++ strikes a good balance between volumetric overlap and 
boundary consistency. 
