To address these challenges, we propose Fisher-Guided
Continuously Adaptive Representation Learning, a unified framework that jointly performs feature-level domain
adaptation and parameter-level knowledge retention (Figure 2). FGCARL consists of two complementary components: Mutual Adaptive Instance Representation and
Fisher-Guided Knowledge Retention. MAIR reduces domainspecific distributional discrepancies while retaining a connection to previously learned feature representations, whereas
FGKR selectively constrains parameters that are important
for previously acquired knowledge. Together, these components establish a stability–plasticity mechanism that enables
the model to adapt to new domains and classes without
requiring rehearsal of historical patient data.
3.2. Mutual Adaptive Instance Representation
Medical images acquired from different institutions may
exhibit substantial distributional differences caused by imaging modalities, acquisition protocols, scanners, reconstruction procedures, and intensity characteristics. Such differences can propagate through the segmentation network and
result in inconsistent intermediate feature representations.
Directly adapting the model to the newly encountered domain may therefore cause previously learned feature statistics to be overwritten.
To address this issue, we propose Mutual Adaptive Instance Representation (MAIR) (Figure 3), which separates
domain-dependent feature statistics from the underlying feature structure and enables bidirectional adaptation between
