MAIR consists of two complementary operations: Forward
MAIR and Backward MAIR. Forward MAIR normalizes the
feature representation of the newly encountered domain and
applies learnable affine transformations to retain the flexibility required for domain-specific adaptation. Backward
MAIR subsequently reconstructs the representation using
statistics associated with a previously learned domain. In this
way, MAIR provides a mechanism for adapting to new feature distributions while maintaining statistical consistency
with previously acquired representations.
Importantly, MAIR operates directly on intermediate
feature representations and therefore does not require storing
or replaying historical patient images or annotations. Its
feature-level formulation also makes it applicable to different segmentation architectures, including convolutional
neural networks (CNNs) and vision transformers (ViTs).
