Therefore, increasing the number of matched moments constrains the two feature distributions over an increasingly
rich family of polynomial statistics. Importantly, matching a
finite number of moments does not imply equality of the underlying distributions. Instead, it provides a practical approximation for reducing higher-order distributional discrepancies, while equality of all moments can uniquely characterize
a distribution under appropriate moment-determinacy conditions.
3.4. Embedding-Level Distribution Consistency
While higher-order moment matching provides a flexible
mechanism for reducing statistical discrepancies between
feature distributions, it does not directly measure the overall
discrepancy between the resulting embeddings. In particular,
finite-order moment matching constrains only a finite set of
statistical characteristics and may leave residual representation drift during incremental adaptation.
