Therefore, parameters with large Fisher information receive
stronger constraints and remain closer to their previously
learned values, whereas parameters with small Fisher information can undergo larger updates to accommodate newly
encountered domains and classes. FGKR consequently establishes an adaptive stability–plasticity trade-off rather than
uniformly restricting all model parameters.
For sequential learning across multiple domains and
classes, the Fisher information and corresponding reference
parameters can be accumulated across incremental stages
according to the implementation of the proposed framework.
This allows historical knowledge to be retained throughout
successive domain and class transitions without requiring
replay of the corresponding historical patient data.
Unlike rehearsal-based approaches, FGKR preserves
historical knowledge through parameter-level constraints
rather than storing previous images or annotations. This
property is particularly advantageous in clinical applications
where historical patient data may be inaccessible because
of privacy, security, or institutional data-governance constraints.
3.6. Unified Incremental Learning Objective
Function
FGCARL jointly optimizes segmentation, distribution
alignment, and knowledge retention at each incremental
stage. The overall objective is defined as
(𝑡+1)
