design space, tuned independently, place the same eight sets on the same side.
A secondary result runs against expectation. The plain U-Net is ahead of the residual variant
on seven of the eight out-of-distribution sets and behind on one, ISIC 2017* by 0.002, and five
differences exceed the floor (Table 7). Under the conditions here, two-dimensional and binary
with 479 to 2,075 training images, the residual variant trained under the nnU-Net recipe therefore
did not outperform its plain counterpart, and out of distribution it is the weaker of the two. Two
qualifications apply: the comparison is single-seed, so it rests on the five differences clearing
the floor, and in polyp and breast the architectures ran at different learning rates, so part of
the difference there may originate in the optimiser rather than the residual connection. Skin is
the exception and is therefore the most informative of the three: both architectures ran at 3e-3,
making that comparison a single-variable ablation, and the plain variant is ahead on two of its
three test sets. What the comparison establishes is that no reasonable task-specific configuration
closes the gap.
The internal comparison shows the deficit does not depend on the architecture chosen; it does
not show either is strong in absolute terms. Because polyp follows the protocol of Fan et al. [23],
published scores are directly comparable in setup (Table 9).
The baselines exceed the published U-Net by 0.226 on CVC-ColonDB and 0.234 on ETIS for
the plain variant, and both are ahead of ACSNet on CVC-ColonDB. Since the plain U-Net and
the published one are close in architecture, almost all of that difference is attributable to the
training recipe rather than the model, which is the effect Isensee et al. [29] identify: the recipe
yields a baseline more than twenty Dice points stronger than the conventional one.
