where ϕk compensates for upsampling information loss.
During training, DLHT randomly selects either the continuous latent f or
the discrete latent ˆf with equal probability to feed into the decoder. The
primary objective of this strategy is to empower the decoder with the capacity to
reconstruct continuous features, thereby transcending the fidelity limits of finite
VQ codebooks. By ensuring representational compatibility between both latents,
this alignment allows the decoder to seamlessly integrate the predicted discrete
latent ˆf (provided by autoregressive transformer) with the residual approximation
ˆf ′ (provided by residual refiner) during inference to generate a denoised result
ˆINDCT that restores fine-grained details.
2.3
Autoregressive Transformer with Residual Refiner
LDCT Prefix Tokens. The original VAR model addresses class-to-image
generation by prepending a class token C to the discrete token map sequence.
To adapt this architecture for LDCT denoising, the primary challenge lies in
effectively injecting the LDCT source information into the NDCT token prediction
process of NDCT discrete token maps. To address this, we employ a pyramid-style
encoder that maps the input ILDCT into LDCT prefix tokens (Fig. 1 (b)),
denoted as rLDCT . This feature map is designed with a spatial resolution of
h × w, explicitly matching the final-scale token map rK. Furthermore, we discard
the standard class token in favor of a trainable task token T, which explicitly",pdf_text,2717,425
