5
guides the network toward the denoising objective. The concatenated sequence
(concat(rLDCT , T), r1, r2, . . . , rK) then serves as the input to the autoregressive
transformer.
During training, a scale-wise attention [12] mask enforces causality by restricting each token map rk to attend only to its preceding scales r≤k. Notably, the
conditional inputs rLDCT and T remain globally visible across all scales, providing stable and continuous LDCT contextual priors. The transformer models the
joint distribution of the NDCT discrete token maps as a product of conditional
probabilities:
p(r1, r2, . . . , rK) =
K
Y
k=1
p(rk | concat(rLDCT , T), r1, r2, . . . , rk−1).
(3)
Residual Refiner. In the standard VAR architecture, the image is reconstructed
by feeding the transformer-predicted discrete latent ˆf directly into the VQVAE
decoder. However, quantization inherently discards critical high-frequency continuous details, yielding reconstructed images with compromised fidelity. To address
this, [15] introduced residual diffusion to model the discrepancy between the
continuous and discrete latents, formally defined as the residual latent f ′ = f −ˆf.
Inspired by [15, 22], we introduce a residual refiner (Fig. 1 (b))-a lightweight
diffusion-based MLP [21]-to estimate this residual latent ˆf ′ from gaussian noise zt,
conditioned on both the final-scale discrete latent ˆzN and the corresponding lastlayer hidden states sK of the transformer. The training objective is formulated
as:
L(sK, ˆzN, f ′) = Eϵ∼N (0,1),t
h
∥ϵ −R (zt | t, sK, ˆzN)∥2i
,
(4)
where ϵ denotes the sampled noise, t is the timestep and R represents the
mapping function of the residual refiner. During inference, the predicted discrete
latent ˆf and ˆf ′ are jointly processed by the hybrid NDCT decoder D to reconstruct
more details-preserved NDCT as:
ˆINDCT = D( ˆf + ˆf ′).
(5)
3
Experiments
3.1
Experimental Setup
Datasets. We evaluate our DeVAR framework using two publicly available
datasets: the 2016 NIH-AAPM-Mayo Clinic Low-Dose CT Grand Challenge
(Mayo-2016) [17] and the ""Low Dose CT Image and Projection Data"" released in
2020 (Mayo-2020) [18]. Mayo-2016 contains 5,936 paired 1mm-thick abdominal
CT slices from 10 patients; we use 8 patients (4,800 pairs) for training and the
remaining 2 (1,136 pairs) for testing. Mayo-2020 comprises 299 scans from 100
patients across two vendors; for this study, we randomly select 2,780 image pairs
from 16 patients for training and 637 pairs from 4 patients for evaluation.",pdf_text,2570,407
