Abbreviations: AAL, ambient assisted living; RPM, remote patient monitoring. 
Searches were conducted in Scopus, PubMed/MEDLINE, and IEEE Xplore using three 
positive query blocks covering remote care and assistive robotics domains, decision–action 
coupling, and architecture/interoperability. A fourth block excluded recurrently retrieved records 
from clearly unrelated domains, such as industrial automation, agriculture, home security, 
autonomous vehicles, warehouses, airports, and surgical robotics. Searches were limited to 
English-language peer-reviewed journal articles and conference papers published from 2018 to 
2026. The searches identified 516 records (317 from Scopus, 113 from PubMed/MEDLINE, and 
86 from IEEE Xplore). After duplicate removal, 448 unique records entered screening. Databaselevel yields, deduplication procedures, and the complete source selection flow are reported in 
Supplemental Tables 1, 2, and 6. 
Screening and eligibility assessment were performed independently and blinded by two 
reviewers (I.R. and L.G.) using prespecified inclusion and exclusion criteria. Full texts were 
consulted whenever eligibility could not be determined from the title and abstract alone. Uncertain 
or discordant cases were resolved through consensus under the supervision of a third reviewer 
(F.C.). Preconsensus agreement was substantial (Cohen’s κ = 0.82). Reviewer-specific screening 
outcomes, the preconsensus contingency table, agreement statistics, and consensus resolution 
details are provided in Supplemental Tables 7–11. 
The final evidence-mapping corpus included 113 records: 93 core system papers used for 
corpus-level synthesis and 20 background papers used for conceptual, regulatory, clinical, and 
robotics framing. The source selection process is summarized in Figure 1. Sections within the 
Supplemental Material provides complete search strings (Section 4), eligibility criteria (Section",pdf_text,2423,302
