tools, making it a cornerstone of Industry 4.0 (47). There are two
primary layers in DT technology (29): and (48).
2.4.1 Layer 1: physical devices
This foundational layer comprises actuators, sensors, and other
hardware elements embedded within the physical system. These
devices continuously gather real-time data, such as temperature,
pressure, speed, or vibration, to produce an accurate digital
representation. For example, in a manufacturing plant, sensors
installed
on
conveyor
belts
capture
operational
data
for
performance monitoring and fault detection. This layer serves as
the sensory system of the DT, directly interfacing with the realworld entity (49).
2.4.2 Layer 2: data source layer
Layer 2 speciﬁes the data sources connected to the physical
entity, facilitating structured data acquisition. It organizes raw
data streams produced by Layer 1 to prepare them for processing
in
the
following
layers.
This
layer
also
facilitates
device
management, ensuring the synchronization and functionality of
sensors and actuators. Examples include IoT gateways that
aggregate data from different sensors.
