an offline training stage using historical medical records, ensuring that its recommendations remain within the boundaries
of established clinical practices [6]. Second, during real-time
operation, the tool suggests treatments by pooling insights
from multiple internal models and only requests human expert
guidance when these models show high uncertainty. This
uncertainty is quantified by measuring the variation in predictions across the model ensemble, providing a reliable measure
of confidence for clinical decision support [7, 8, 9]. Third,
the AI system performs frequent, stable updates on recent
patient data to adapt to evolving conditions without reacting
erratically to new information [10]. To minimize the workload
for clinicians, an automated selection process identifies only
the most informative and diverse cases for expert review [11].
The tool exposes lightweight controls for query threshold,
stream rate, and batch size, enabling simple, rapid runtime
behavior changes without requiring full retraining.
This paper presents an online adaptive decision support
framework that integrates TE estimation, DT, and RL into
an online adaptive decision support tool. First, we establish
a robust decision-making core by combining a stable model
initialized from historical data with a responsive online update
