fit to observed trajectories via gradient-based system identification, often in
a per-instance manner [52]. Whether the mechanistic and neural components
can be separately identified when given indirect observations, and to what
extent this may affect the predictive performance of the optimized hybrid
model, have not been well investigated.
Amortized inference strategies have emerged for optimizing hybrid models.
Hybrid-VAE [41] combines mechanistic dynamics with latent neural
components and use an encoder to infer the latent parameters for both components from indirect observations. Robust hybrid learning [46] improves
this by generating expert-augmented training examples using the approxi10",pdf_text,2299,309
