Existing works in DT optimization are mostly based on the maximization
of this likelihood, with learnable parameters (e.g., zPHY and zNN) inferred
from x0:T. This corresponds to the inference of a generative model whose
latent variables (zPHY and zNN) have an unconditional prior. As established
by the theory of nonlinear independent component analysis (ICA) [21], the
neural latent variable zNN of such an unconditional generative model is not
identifiable [51]: while we refer the audience to [21, 51] for theoretical details,
intuitively it means that an arbitrary nonlinear transformation can change
the value of zNN without changing its distribution, and this transformation
can be absorbed by the neural function without affecting the decoding to
x0:T. Furthermore, this lack of identifiability in zNN may then compromise
the recovery of zPHY, i.e., multiple values of zPHY and zNN may produce
observationally equivalent p(x0:T | s) [51]: while these models may perform
similarly in reconstructing x0:T, their ability to predict beyond x0:T may be
impacted .
