3.4. Identifiability Theory
Built on the theory established in [51], we can establish the identifiability
of the latent neural parameter zNN of the conditional hybrid DT.
Theorem 1. Assume that we observed data p(x0:T) generated following the
process defined by Equations (10)-(12) with parameters (ϕ, ψ) and latent variable p(zNN|Dc) defined as in Equation (11). Denote this generation process
as x0:T = G(zNN). Assume a model with parameter (ˆϕ, ˆψ) and p(ˆzNN|Dc)
generates p(ˆx0:T) that is observationally equivalent to p(x0:T). Assume the
following hold:
1. The set {x0:T ∈X|φε(x0:T) = 0} has measure zero, where φε is the
characteristic function of the density pε(xt −Hut).
2. The function G is injective.
3. There exist de+1 distinct context sets Ds,0, Ds,1, . . . , Ds,de such that the
de × de matrix L defined as follows is invertible
L = (λ(Dc,1) −λ(Dc,0), . . . ,λ(Dc,de) −λ(Dc,0))
(14)
where λ denotes the parameters of p(zNN|Dc), d the dimension of zNN,
and e the dimension of its sufficient statistics T.
then there exists an invertible de × de matrix A and vector b, such that
T(zNN) = AT(ˆzNN) + b, i.e., T(zNN) are identifiable up to affine transformations.
The proof of Theorem 1 directly builds on that in [51]. Interestingly,
condition 3 in Theorem 1 specifies that the recovery of the latent variable
zNN requires sufficient variability of its values, as conditioned on Dc, to be
observed. In other words, for a Gaussian distribution used in this paper, a
minimum of 2d + 1 unique values of zNN need to be present in the observed
data in order for it to be identified.
4. Synthetic Data Experiments
We first evaluated HAPI-EP trained and tested on synthetic data generated on different bi-ventricular models.
