than the supervised baselines without task-specific training. On 30-Day Readmission, MedJev and the
supervised baselines achieve AUROCs between 0.65 and 0.69. The difference between XGBoost and
MedJev is +0.012 (95% CI -0.028 to +0.053), indicating no statistically significant improvement
from supervision at this label budget.
Supervised models perform better on several operational outcomes. On ED Triage Admission, XGBoost
exceeds MedJev by +0.064 AUROC (95% CI +0.034 to +0.093). The corresponding difference for
ED Admission at 2 h is +0.081 (95% CI +0.053 to +0.113). On ED Triage Disposition, XGBoost
achieves an accuracy improvement of +8.0 percentage points (95% CI +4.9 to +11.1). For Length
of Stay > 7 Days, TF-IDF exceeds MedJev by +0.045 AUROC (95% CI +0.011 to +0.080). These
results indicate that supervised models are more effective for outcomes associated with local hospital
operations.
Among zero-shot models, MedJev outperforms its base model on In-Hospital Mortality and Length
of Stay > 7 Days, with paired differences of -0.027 and -0.024, respectively. However, Qwen3.5-9B
performs better on ED Admission at 2 h, with a difference of +0.026. Qwen3.5-2B achieves an AUROC
of 0.711 on In-Hospital Mortality, 0.501 on 30-Day Readmission, and 0.421 on Length of Stay >
7 Days. Pythia-2.8B achieves AUROCs of 0.764 and 0.629 on In-Hospital Mortality and Length of
Stay > 7 Days, respectively, but performs near chance on ED Triage Admission (0.490). Its accuracy
on ED Triage Disposition is 4.2%. Overall, MedJev achieves stronger performance than the smaller
zero-shot models on most patient-level tasks, although System-One training does not consistently
improve performance over its base model.
4.3. Probability Calibration
We evaluate whether the probabilities produced by MedJev correspond to observed outcome frequencies. Figures 4 and 5 present expected calibration error and calibration curves across the benchmarks.
MedJev is well calibrated on medical knowledge benchmarks, with ECE values of 0.039 on MedQA,
0.028 on USMLE, and 0.063 on NEJM. In contrast, its zero-shot probabilities are poorly calibrated on
patient-level outcomes. For In-Hospital Mortality, the mean predicted probability is 0.312, compared
with an observed mortality rate of 2.6%, resulting in an ECE of 0.286. The corresponding ECE values
are 0.191 for Length of Stay > 7 Days, 0.272 for 30-Day Readmission, and 0.147 for ED Triage
Admission. These results indicate that good risk discrimination does not necessarily imply calibrated
outcome probabilities in the zero-shot setting.
We apply post-hoc recalibration using labeled examples from the corresponding training partitions.
Recalibration reduces ECE to 0.007 on In-Hospital Mortality, 0.027 on Length of Stay > 7 Days, 0.024
on 30-Day Readmission, and 0.041 on ED Triage Admission. These values are comparable to those
obtained by XGBoost, which achieves ECE values of 0.014, 0.059, 0.046, and 0.042 on the same tasks.
Recalibration changes the probability estimates without changing the reported AUROC.
