Figure 4 | Expected calibration error across the ten benchmarks for zero-shot MedJev, recalibrated MedJev, Qwen3.5-9B, and XGBoost. Lower values indicate better calibration.
We also examine the effect of System-One training on calibration. Qwen3.5-9B has ECE values of
0.426, 0.348, and 0.372 on In-Hospital Mortality, Length of Stay > 7 Days, and 30-Day Readmission,
respectively. These values are higher than those of MedJev, indicating that System-One training
improves probability calibration even when local recalibration remains necessary.
Calibration parameters do not consistently transfer across benchmarks. Applying a temperature fitted
on MedQA training questions changes ECE from 0.028 to 0.038 on USMLE and from 0.063 to 0.061 on
NEJM. On DiagnosisArena, ECE increases from 0.071 to 0.112. These results indicate that calibration
fitted on one benchmark does not necessarily improve probability estimates on another.
4.4. Effect of Local Training Data
We vary the number of labeled training examples from 25 to 1,000 to assess the data requirements of
recalibration and supervised learning (fig. 6). For MedJev, we fit post-hoc calibration parameters
using each label budget while keeping the model predictions fixed. For the supervised baselines, we
train separate models using the corresponding labeled examples.
Recalibration improves probability estimates with relatively few labeled examples. On In-Hospital
Mortality, Platt scaling reduces ECE from 0.286 to approximately 0.03 using 50 labeled examples.
With 200 examples, ECE ranges from 0.014 to 0.064 across the binary patient-level benchmarks.
